1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,744
This is a technical demonstration of Before the Merge, a pipeline

2
00:00:04,744 --> 00:00:09,271
that collects recent sources, uses an AI model for editorial decisions,

3
00:00:09,271 --> 00:00:13,160
and produces a three part series for technical leads of small

4
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:17,432
software teams deciding how much work to delegate to coding agents.

5
00:00:19,282 --> 00:00:22,476
The topic, the sources, the editorial policy and the

6
00:00:22,476 --> 00:00:26,407
four model prompts live in their prescribed configuration files.

7
00:00:26,757 --> 00:00:31,139
The sources cover RSS feeds, YouTube videos and podcasts, from

8
00:00:31,139 --> 00:00:35,521
independent publishers, and every run takes a snapshot of this

9
00:00:35,521 --> 00:00:39,621
configuration so later edits never rewrite an earlier run.

10
00:00:41,471 --> 00:00:45,487
The entry point is run_local.sh, which sets up

11
00:00:45,487 --> 00:00:48,980
the local Ollama model and calls run.sh.

12
00:00:49,330 --> 00:00:51,485
Run dot S H only coordinates.

13
00:00:51,835 --> 00:00:56,579
It calls the collection workflow, which runs the three collectors, extraction, the

14
00:00:56,579 --> 00:01:00,686
evidence store and the thirty day filter, and then the series workflow,

15
00:01:00,686 --> 00:01:05,083
which runs selection, briefs, articles, video scripts and the content check.

16
00:01:05,433 --> 00:01:09,951
Each of the sixteen required scripts owns one job, reads and writes

17
00:01:09,951 --> 00:01:13,796
JSON Lines, and writes a receipt to the run's log folder.

18
00:01:14,146 --> 00:01:18,007
Only store.sh opens the SQLite evidence index.

19
00:01:18,357 --> 00:01:22,472
The pipeline stops at awaiting review, and packaging happens only

20
00:01:22,472 --> 00:01:26,144
after a human approval that matches the current review_id.

21
00:01:27,993 --> 00:01:32,642
The first live run, on the fifteenth of October, found twenty eight eligible

22
00:01:32,642 --> 00:01:37,658
items, eighteen written, five podcast and five YouTube, from ten publisher groups.

23
00:01:38,007 --> 00:01:41,755
The model accepted twenty five, rejected two and deferred one,

24
00:01:41,755 --> 00:01:45,261
then wrote three briefs, three articles and three scripts.

25
00:01:45,611 --> 00:01:48,113
The content check passed, I reviewed the drafts,

26
00:01:48,113 --> 00:01:50,667
wrote the approval, and the package was produced.

27
00:01:52,517 --> 00:01:55,461
Here is one published claim traced back to its evidence.

28
00:01:55,811 --> 00:02:01,123
Article one quotes Jake Cukjati saying the agents test everything and implement it.

29
00:02:01,473 --> 00:02:07,029
The brief records that as claim 01-c1, a source claim, pointing

30
00:02:07,029 --> 00:02:12,673
to the YouTube item, an exact version hash and segment s-000096.

31
00:02:13,023 --> 00:02:17,119
The decisions file shows the item was accepted for pieces one and two.

32
00:02:17,469 --> 00:02:20,874
The stored version shows the original publication date, the

33
00:02:20,874 --> 00:02:23,933
ninth of October, separately from the retrieval date.

34
00:02:24,283 --> 00:02:26,691
And the retained transcript gives the exact

35
00:02:26,691 --> 00:02:28,763
words at six minutes and ten seconds.

36
00:02:30,613 --> 00:02:32,735
The second live run used the next

37
00:02:32,735 --> 00:02:35,114
UTC date and the same evidence store.

38
00:02:35,464 --> 00:02:38,651
Twenty items were recognised as unchanged and were not

39
00:02:38,651 --> 00:02:41,779
processed again, and there were no duplicate records.

40
00:02:42,129 --> 00:02:47,101
Three items published on the fifteenth were detected as new: two GitHub changelog

41
00:02:47,101 --> 00:02:51,644
posts, which the model accepted, and a Cloudflare post, which it deferred.

42
00:02:51,994 --> 00:02:55,248
The thirty day window moved by one day, but the oldest

43
00:02:55,248 --> 00:02:59,226
item is from the nineteenth of September, so nothing was excluded.

44
00:03:01,076 --> 00:03:04,532
The system also reports failures instead of hiding them.

45
00:03:04,882 --> 00:03:10,386
In an earlier collection attempt, the GitHub feed contained XML the parser rejected.

46
00:03:10,736 --> 00:03:13,478
The feed collector exited with an error, the run

47
00:03:13,478 --> 00:03:17,136
was marked incomplete, and the other collectors still completed.

48
00:03:17,486 --> 00:03:20,437
After the parser was fixed, the retry was recorded

49
00:03:20,437 --> 00:03:23,566
separately and the original failure receipt was kept.

50
00:03:23,916 --> 00:03:27,682
A labelled replay of the saved inputs produced no new and no

51
00:03:27,682 --> 00:03:32,641
changed items, which shows the store handles repeated input without duplicates.

52
00:03:34,491 --> 00:03:37,834
I reviewed three accepted and three rejected or deferred

53
00:03:37,834 --> 00:03:41,595
decisions against the source passages, and disagreed with four.

54
00:03:41,945 --> 00:03:47,496
One correction mattered most: a brief presented a sponsor advertisement for Blitzy,

55
00:03:47,496 --> 00:03:52,846
claiming 80% of software epics are delivered autonomously, as a podcast finding.

56
00:03:53,196 --> 00:03:55,522
I relabelled it as an advertisement.

57
00:03:55,872 --> 00:04:00,096
I then revised the policy and reran selection on the same items.

58
00:04:00,446 --> 00:04:04,853
The policy changed the counts, but it did not reliably fix the reviewed

59
00:04:04,853 --> 00:04:08,702
cases, and run two even repeated a vendor claim I had removed.

60
00:04:09,052 --> 00:04:10,801
So run two was not published.

61
00:04:11,151 --> 00:04:16,207
The real bottleneck is excerpt retrieval, and human review remains a required step.

62
00:04:18,057 --> 00:04:21,318
The main architectural tradeoff is immutable, content

63
00:04:21,318 --> 00:04:24,457
addressed evidence behind a single store interface.

64
00:04:24,807 --> 00:04:28,316
It costs extra storage and validation, and conservative

65
00:04:28,316 --> 00:04:31,762
identity matching leaves some copies for human review.

66
00:04:32,112 --> 00:04:36,123
In return, every published claim can be traced to the exact passage

67
00:04:36,123 --> 00:04:40,794
that supported it, and running the pipeline again does not duplicate anything.

68
00:04:41,144 --> 00:04:45,027
The three articles and their videos are published and linked to each other.

69
00:04:45,377 --> 00:04:46,721
Thank you for watching.

